    "license": "city-published-permission-pending",
    "digitizedDate": "2026-08-20",
    "notes": "First police call-taking pack. Grounded on the manual's emergency-line procedure ('911, what is the emergency?' -> What happened / When (in progress, just occurred, or cold) / Where (verify; house or apartment) / Who (suspects described top-down) / Weapons (kind; never assume)), its always-ask questions (suspect there now? weapon? vehicle - including the burglary-in-progress driveway-vehicle question), and its dispatch priorities quoted from the manual: PRIORITY 1 = immediate danger to life or property, all felonies in progress or just occurred (within 10 minutes); PRIORITY 2 = serious trouble may exist, no immediate emergency (examples: misdemeanor in progress, suspicious person 912P, disturbance 415, alarms); PRIORITY 3 = cold reports, no danger to life or property. Injury accidents (901A) are among the problem codes 'automatically a priority one' per the manual (its PRIORITY 2 example list also shows 901A; the automatic-P1 rule is the more specific statement and is used here), and fire/medical emergencies are screened and transferred within 30 seconds - modeled as an immediate-transfer protocol. Editorial: question phrasings are condensed from the manual's prose (it is a training manual, not a card set); the domestic-violence questions (physical or verbal? can you speak freely?) come from its DV section. Keep-caller-on-line, reassure-units-on-the-way, and caller-safety post-dispatch lines are from the manual's call-handling rules. License: City of Alameda government work published on the city site; written-permission request tracked in AccesSOS/infra (same posture as the NJ pack). SIMULATION ONLY - never for live call-taking. Digitized from pages 42-44 (call-taking), 55-59 (incident mask samples), and 176-178 (dispatch priorities) of the manual. Safety-first default: for felony calls (burglary, robbery) PRIORITY_3 requires an explicitly cold timeframe; an unparsed timeframe defaults to PRIORITY_1, consistent with the corpus convention that unknown always falls to the safer tier."
  },
